<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuzi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nuzi was an ancient Hurrian town near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkuk">Kirkuk</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets found there document its society and economy in the mid-second millennium BCE. It was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a>, illustrating Mitanni's control over the region east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuzi.jpg" alt="Nuzi" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zunkir · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meso2mil.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nuzi, also known by its Akkadian name Gasur, stood at the modern site of Yorghan Tepe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, 12 kilometres south-west of Arrapha, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkuk">Kirkuk</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. It was occupied from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid period</a> in the 5th millennium BCE until late in the 2nd millennium BCE, and again, after a period of abandonment, in the Parthian era. The main mound, about 200 metres square, was protected by a city wall with gates, and a well-drained main road ran between the central temples and the palace. Houses lay on two rises to the north, within an outer town with its own wall, now destroyed by modern farming.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> the city was probably known as Gasur and was the seat of a governor, known from a clay sealing found at Tell Brak. Of 222 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets of this period, written in Old Akkadian, most date to the reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> and Shar-kali-sharri. They show a thriving commercial centre with trading contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>. The Nuzi map, found here, is the oldest known map. About 6 by 6.5 centimetres, it shows a canal or river, two mountain ranges and a city called Maskan-dur-ebla, but it is unknown exactly what it depicts. After a sack the site was occupied less densely under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a>, Isin-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> and Old Babylonian periods.</p><p>In the middle of the 2nd millennium BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrians">Hurrians</a> took control of the town and renamed it Nuzi. A sealing found there names Saustatar, son of Parsatatar, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>. In the 15th and 14th centuries BCE it was a small provincial town of Arrapha in an area populated mostly by Hurrians, its central complex holding two temples, to Šawuška or Ištar and to Teššub, and a palace administered by a governor. Most worship was in the household, with elements of ancestor worship in which the eldest son inherited the family cult statue. After the fall of the Mitanni kingdom Nuzi went into gradual decline. The excavators dated its destruction to about 1500 BCE, but later work proposes about 1430 to 1330 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tablets from Yorghan Tepe appeared as early as 1896, and serious excavation began in 1925 after Gertrude Bell noticed tablets in the markets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Work continued through 1931. About 5,000 tablets are known, mostly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-institute">Oriental Institute</a>, the Harvard Semitic Museum and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-iraq">Iraq Museum</a>. Those from Nuzi are in a dialect of Akkadian influenced by Hurrian, and about a quarter concern the business of a single family, that of Tehip-tilla and Surki-tilla.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nuzi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the reign of Shaushtatar (c. 1450 BCE), Mitanni was a powerful kingdom able to project military force, as shown by his sack of Ashur and his authority over vassals such as Arrapha, whose archives survive at <b>Nuzi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The best evidence comes from <b>Nuzi</b> (Yorghan Tepe), a provincial town excavated in the 1920s and 1930s, where over 5,000 tablets were found.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-arrapha">Kingdom of Arrapha</a></li></ul></section>
